"Please build a strategy deck storyline for the following brief.\n\nAudience: Halcyon Beverages all-hands (all 340 employees, company-wide)\nDecision: This is an internal communication deck explaining the company's decision to shift from glass to aluminum packaging for its premium sparkling water line. The decision has already been made by leadership. The purpose of the deck is to explain the rationale to employees and answer the questions leadership expects to hear.\n\n---\n**BRIEF: Halcyon Beverages — Packaging Transition Rationale (All-Hands)**\n\nBackground: Halcyon Beverages makes premium sparkling water sold in glass bottles. Glass has been the brand's signature for 11 years. Leadership announced on November 10, 2026, that the flagship line will transition to aluminum cans beginning with the Spring 2027 production run.\n\nThe five facts leadership wants communicated:\n\n1. **Cost:** Glass bottles cost $0.31 per unit to procure; aluminum cans cost $0.14 per unit. At 2026 production volume of 48M units, the annual saving is $8.16M. This saving funds the new retail distribution investment.\n\n2. **Sustainability:** Aluminum has 73% post-consumer recycled content on average in the North American supply chain vs. glass at 27%. The company's Scope 3 emissions model (conducted by third-party auditor Greenfield Analytics) projects a 34% reduction in packaging-related Scope 3 emissions per unit.\n\n3. **Retail access:** Three major retail chains (Costco, Kroger, and Target — here called Chain A, Chain B, Chain C for the storyline) have confirmed they will expand Halcyon's shelf space in their canned-water sections but cannot accommodate additional glass SKUs.\n\n4. **Convenience:** Internal consumer research (n=1,800, online panel, October 2026) shows 61% of Halcyon buyers prefer aluminum for on-the-go consumption; only 29% prefer glass for the same occasion.\n\n5. **Brand continuity:** Leadership is committed to maintaining the premium positioning: can design has been developed with the original brand agency; the same water source, filtration, and carbonation process remain unchanged.\n\n---\nNote A: The consumer research was conducted online; the sample overrepresents urban and suburban consumers vs. Halcyon's actual buyer mix, which skews rural and exurban (per the company's own CRM data). Leadership is aware of this limitation.\n\nNote B: One founding-era employee has circulated an internal memo arguing that glass is \"the soul of the brand\" and that the transition will cost Halcyon its premium identity. Leadership does not agree with this assessment but wants the deck to acknowledge the concern without dismissing it.\n\n---\nFull storyline please: governing thought, argument tree, action-title slide list, per-slide specs, and self-check."